{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'stripe whoami 2>&1 || true\nstripe sandbox create --help 2>&1 | head -160\nstripe keys --help 2>&1 | head -200\nstripe projects init --help 2>&1 | head -180\ncurl -sS https://api.sandbox.prodigi.com/swagger/v4.0/swagger.json -H \"X-API-Key: [REDACTED_SECRET]\" | head -c 500 || true'","aggregated_output":"Profile:        default\nAuthenticated:  false\nRun `stripe login` to authenticate.\nCreate a new Stripe sandbox with test API keys.\n\nIf you are already logged in (have a configured API key), opens the\nsandbox management page in your browser instead.\n\nOtherwise, uses a proof-of-work challenge to provision a temporary sandbox\nwithout authentication. If that fails, automatically falls back to\nbrowser-based signup/login.\n\nKeys are saved to the current CLI profile so subsequent stripe commands\nwork immediately.\n\nUsage:\n  stripe sandbox create [flags]\n\nExamples:\n  stripe sandbox create --email you@example.com\n  stripe sandbox create --from-git\n\n[Agent guidance]\n  Provisions a sandbox and saves keys to the current CLI profile.\n  Pass --from-git to resolve your email from git config user.email.\n  Pass --email to provide an explicit email address.\n  Falls back to browser login on server errors.\n  If you do not have an account: run stripe sandbox create (provisions a claimable sandbox without a browser).\n  If you already have a key: use --api-key (or set STRIPE_API_KEY).\n  Run stripe --map to quickly see all available commands.\n  Run stripe resources to discover all available API resources.\n  Run stripe [resource] --help to see operations and required/common parameters for a resource.\n  Run stripe help [resource] [operation] to see the full parameter list for a specific operation.\n\n\nFlags:\n      --email string       Your email address\n      --from-git           Infer email and full name from git config\n      --full-name string   Your full name (optional)\n  -h, --help               help for create\n      --non-interactive    Print output directly without waiting for input\n\nGlobal flags:\n      --api-key string        Your API key to use for the command\n      --color string          turn on/off color output (on, off, auto)\n      --config string         config file (default is\n                              $HOME/.config/stripe/config.toml)\n      --device-name string    device name\n      --log-level string      log level (debug, info, trace, warn, error)\n                              (default \"info\")\n      --map string[=\"tree\"]   Print a command tree [tree|compact|paths|json]\n  -p, --project-name string   the project name to read from for config\n                              (default \"default\")\nManage API keys\n\nUsage:\n\n[Agent guidance]\n  If you do not have an account: run stripe sandbox create (provisions a claimable sandbox without a browser).\n  If you already have a key: use --api-key (or set STRIPE_API_KEY).\n  Run stripe --map to quickly see all available commands.\n  Run stripe resources to discover all available API resources.\n  Run stripe [resource] --help to see operations and required/common parameters for a resource.\n  Run stripe help [resource] [operation] to see the full parameter list for a specific operation.\n\n\nFlags:\n  -h, --help   help for keys\n\nGlobal flags:\n      --api-key string        Your API key to use for the command\n      --color string          turn on/off color output (on, off, auto)\n      --config string         config file (default is\n                              $HOME/.config/stripe/config.toml)\n      --device-name string    device name\n      --log-level string      log level (debug, info, trace, warn, error)\n                              (default \"info\")\n      --map string[=\"tree\"]   Print a command tree [tree|compact|paths|json]\n  -p, --project-name string   the project name to read from for config\n                              (